The '''2009 UEFA Champions League final''' was played on 27 May 2009 at the [[Stadio Olimpico]] in Rome, Italy. The match determined the winners of the [[2008–09 UEFA Champions League|2008–09 season]] of the [[UEFA Champions League]], a tournament for the top [[association football|football]] clubs in Europe. The match was won by [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] of Spain, who beat England's [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] 2–0. [[Samuel Eto'o]] opened the scoring in the 10th minute, and [[Lionel Messi]] added another goal 20&nbsp;minutes from the end to earn Barcelona a historic [[Continental Treble|treble]] of [[La Liga]], the [[Copa del Rey]] and the Champions League, a feat never before achieved by a Spanish club.<ref name="guardiola_salutes">{{cite news |first=Kevin |last=Ashby |title=Guardiola salutes his treble winners |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/match/302813--barcelona-vs-man-utd/ |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=27 May 2009 |access-date=28 May 2009}}</ref> This was the only UEFA Champions League final where [[Messi–Ronaldo rivalry|Lionel Messi and Cristiano Ronaldo]] have met, as well as the last match Ronaldo played for Manchester United until his return to the club in 2021. The match was refereed by Swiss [[referee (association football)|referee]] [[Massimo Busacca]].<ref name="referee"/>

This was Barcelona's third victory in the competition, 17 years after their first European Cup win in [[1992 European Cup final|1992]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Andrew |last=Haslam |title=Spain savour European pre-eminence |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01d9-0e723fb00248-203b61703893-1000--spain-savour-european-pre-eminence/ |publisher=UEFA |date=27 May 2009 |access-date=28 May 2009}}</ref> Manchester United went into the match as the competition's defending champions, the first title holders to reach the final since [[Juventus FC|Juventus]] in [[1997 UEFA Champions League final|1997]]. Manchester United also sought to be the first team to retain the European Cup since the [[AC Milan|Milan]] win in [[1990 European Cup final|1990]]. It was the fifth consecutive year that the final involved at least one English team.<ref name="rome_ready">{{cite news |title=Rome ready to welcome European superpowers |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01d9-0e722accf133-5923432425c3-1000--rome-ready-to-welcome-european-superpowers/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110604141159/http://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/matches/season%3D2009/round%3D15280/match%3D302813/prematch/background/index.html |url-status=live |archive-date=4 June 2011 |publisher=UEFA |date=26 May 2009 |access-date=28 May 2009}}</ref> The Stadio Olimpico had hosted three previous Champions League finals, in [[1977 European Cup final|1977]], [[1984 European Cup final|1984]] and [[1996 UEFA Champions League final|1996]].<ref name="olimpico">{{cite web |title=Stadio Olimpico |url=http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/finals/newsid=739497.html |publisher=UEFA |date=20 March 2009 |access-date=6 May 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140520175228/http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/finals/newsid=739497.html |archive-date=20 May 2014 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

Prequalified as the winners of the 2008–09 Champions League, Barcelona played [[2008–09 UEFA Cup]] winners [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]] in the [[2009 UEFA Super Cup]],<ref name="super_cup">{{cite news |first=Andrew |last=Haslam |title=Pedro pounces to add to Barça glory |url=http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/supercup/fixturesresults/round=2000023/match=2001219/report=rp.html |publisher=UEFA |date=28 August 2009 |access-date=8 September 2009 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100211153548/http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/supercup/fixturesresults/round%3D2000023/match%3D2001219/report%3Drp.html |archive-date=11 February 2010}}</ref> and they represented UEFA at the [[2009 FIFA Club World Cup]].<ref name="club_world_cup">{{cite news |title=Club elite take shape |url=http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/y=2009/m=7/news=club-elite-taking-shape-1083350.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151009033318/http://www.fifa.com/clubworldcup/news/y=2009/m=7/news=club-elite-taking-shape-1083350.html |archive-date=9 October 2015 |publisher=FIFA |date=22 July 2009 |access-date=16 June 2010}}</ref> For winning the final, Barcelona won €7&nbsp;million in prize money, and Manchester United received €4&nbsp;million as runners-up.<ref name="distribution">{{cite journal |date=July 2009 |title=Distribution of revenue to participating clubs |journal=Uefadirect |issue=87 |pages=6–7 |url=https://www.uefa.com/MultimediaFiles/Download/publications/uefa/uefamedia/83/97/47/839747_download.pdf |access-date=26 July 2009}}</ref>
{{TOClimit}}

==Background==
Barcelona and Manchester United had previously faced each other nine times in European competitions: three times in the [[UEFA Cup Winners' Cup]] and six times in the UEFA Champions League. Of those nine matches, Manchester United had recorded three wins to Barcelona's two, with the remaining four matches finishing in draws. The only time that the two teams met in a final came in 1991, when they contested the [[1991 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1991 Cup Winners' Cup final]]. The first meeting between the two sides came in the third round of the [[1983–84 European Cup Winners' Cup|1983–84 Cup Winners' Cup]]; Barcelona won the first leg at the [[Camp Nou]] 2–0, but Manchester United won the return leg 3–0 at [[Old Trafford]] to reach the semi-finals. That result marked Manchester United's biggest win over Barcelona; Barcelona's biggest win was a 4–0 home victory in the group stage of the [[1994–95 UEFA Champions League|1994–95 Champions League]]. The most recent meeting between the teams came in the semi-finals of the [[2007–08 UEFA Champions League|2007–08 Champions League]], when United held Barcelona to a 0–0 draw at the Camp Nou before beating them 1–0 at Old Trafford.<ref>{{cite web |title=United versus Barcelona |url=http://www.stretfordend.co.uk/opponents/barcelona.html |publisher=StretfordEnd.co.uk |access-date=7 May 2009}}</ref> Despite their record against Manchester United, Barcelona had an overall winning record against English clubs, having won 20 and lost 15 of their 52 matches against English opposition. Manchester United, on the other hand, had a losing record against Spanish teams; they had lost 11 and won 10 of their 37 matches.<ref name="rome_ready"/>

Both teams had won the UEFA Champions League before; Manchester United had three titles, while Barcelona had two. The most recent of these had come only the season before, when Manchester United beat [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] on [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]] in the [[Luzhniki Stadium]] in Moscow. They won the first of their European Cups in [[1968 European Cup final|1968]], beating [[S.L. Benfica|Benfica]] 4–1 at [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|Wembley Stadium]], while their second was achieved in [[1999 UEFA Champions League final|1999]] with a 2–1 win over [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] at Barcelona's home ground, the [[Camp Nou]], in which they overturned a 1–0 Bayern lead with two goals in second-half injury time. Barcelona's first European Cup was won as recently as [[1992 European Cup final|1992]], when they beat [[UC Sampdoria|Sampdoria]] 1–0 after [[extra time]] at Wembley; their only other title came in [[2006 UEFA Champions League final|2006]] with a 2–1 win over another English side, [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]]. Prior to 2009, unlike Barcelona, Manchester United had never lost a European Cup final; Barcelona had lost three – in [[1961 European Cup final|1961]], [[1986 European Cup final|1986]] and [[1994 UEFA Champions League final|1994]], to Benfica, [[FCSB|Steaua București]] and [[AC Milan|Milan]] respectively.<ref name="rome_ready"/>

Both teams went into the match as the champions of their respective countries – the first time that the final had been contested by domestic champions since 1999 – both winning with games to spare. Manchester United won their 11th [[Premier League]] title with a 0–0 draw at home to [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] on 16 May,<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Man Utd 0–0 Arsenal |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/8038259.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=16 May 2009 |access-date=17 May 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090516233102/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/8038259.stm |archive-date=16 May 2009 |url-status=live}}</ref> while Barcelona were confirmed as [[La Liga]] champions for the first time in three years when [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] lost to [[Villarreal CF|Villarreal]] on the same day.<ref name="spanish_title">{{cite news |title=Barca clinch Spanish league title |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8054011.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=16 May 2009 |access-date=17 May 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090517012730/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8054011.stm |archive-date=17 May 2009 |url-status=live}}</ref> Both Manchester United and Barcelona were also looking for another Champions League title to cap a season in which they had won multiple trophies: Manchester United had already won four out of a possible seven trophies in [[2008–09 Manchester United F.C. season|2008–09]] and were playing for the possibility of becoming the third team to retain the [[double (association football)#European doubles|European double]] (the domestic league and the European Cup) and win a [[treble (association football)|treble]] along with the [[2008–09 Football League Cup|Football League Cup]], while Barcelona were aiming to become the first Spanish club to win the [[treble (association football)#Continental trebles|continental treble]] of [[2008–09 La Liga|La Liga]], the [[2008–09 Copa del Rey|Copa del Rey]] and the UEFA Champions League.<ref name="spanish_title"/> United also entered the final on a 25-match undefeated streak in the Champions League, a then-competition record.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0243-0e9cafe88e22-3c9a4d092ca1-1000/ |title=Champions League: Longest unbeaten runs, winning streaks and consecutive clean sheets |publisher=UEFA |date=11 December 2024 |access-date=14 December 2024}}</ref>

==Venue==
The [[Stadio Olimpico]] in Rome was selected as the venue for the 2009 UEFA Champions League final at a meeting of the UEFA Executive Committee in [[Ljubljana]], [[Slovenia]], on 4 October 2006. The committee – who decided the venue for the 2008 final and the [[2008 UEFA Cup final|2008]] and [[2009 UEFA Cup final]]s at the same meeting – based their decision on a number of factors, including stadium capacity, safety and security facilities, and accessibility.<ref>{{cite news |first=Mark |last=Chaplin |title=Moscow chosen for 2008 final |url=http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/news/kind=1/newsid=464106.html |publisher=UEFA |location=Ljubljana |date=4 October 2006 |access-date=12 May 2009 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160120233027/http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/news/kind%3D1/newsid%3D464106.html |archive-date=20 January 2016}}</ref>

The Stadio Olimpico had hosted three [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League finals|European Cup finals]] before 2009: the [[1977 European Cup final|1977]] and [[1984 European Cup final|1984 finals]], both of which were won by Liverpool – Liverpool beat [[Borussia Mönchengladbach]] 3–1 in 1977 before beating home side Roma 4–2 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]] after the match finished 1–1 after extra time; the most recent final to be held at the Stadio Olimpico was the [[1996 UEFA Champions League final|1996 final]], which Juventus also won 4–2 on penalties after a 1–1 draw with Ajax.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Eugene |last1=Ravdin |first2=Paolo |last2=Menicucci |title=Moscow and Rome feast on finals |url=http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/news/kind=1/newsid=464251.html |publisher=UEFA |location=Riga & Milan |date=5 October 2006 |access-date=12 May 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160120233027/http://en.archive.uefa.com/competitions/ucl/news/kind=1/newsid=464251.html |archive-date=20 January 2016 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

The construction of the stadium was commissioned by Italian dictator [[Benito Mussolini]] in the mid-1930s as the centrepiece of a new sports complex in the city, to be named ''Foro Mussolini''. After World War II, the complex was renamed ''Foro Italico'' and the stadium was radically redesigned as a 54,000-capacity arena for the [[1960 Summer Olympics]]. After hosting the [[1987 World Championships in Athletics|1987 World Athletics Championships]], the stadium was redeveloped in time for the [[1990 FIFA World Cup]], at which [[Germany national football team|West Germany]]'s 1–0 win over [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] in the [[1990 FIFA World Cup final|final]] was played there. The stadium's most recent renovation, in 2008, brought the capacity to 72,689.<ref name="olimpico"/>

