==Post-match==

=== Trophy presentation ===
[[File:002 men at work UEFA 2009, Rome.jpg|thumb|Manchester United's players look on as their Barcelona counterparts receive their winners' medals.|alt=A line of men in white shirts watching men in blue-and-red shirts standing on a rostrum in the background.]]

At the final whistle, Barcelona manager Pep Guardiola offered his commiserations to each of Manchester United's players, while former Manchester United defender [[Gerard Piqué]] talked with his erstwhile teammates. Both teams then retreated to either end of the stadium to applaud their fans.

[[File:La cara de la derrota.jpg|thumb|left|[[Prince William, Duke of Cambridge|Prince William]] looks on as [[Cristiano Ronaldo]] receives his runners-up medal from [[UEFA]] President [[Michel Platini]].|alt=A man in a white shirt receives a medal from a man in a suit.]]

While match referee [[Massimo Busacca]] and his assistants collected their mementoes of the final, the Barcelona team formed a [[guard of honour]] for the Manchester United players,<ref>Simons, Archibald (1:40:49)</ref> just as the English side had done for [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] in [[2008 UEFA Champions League final|2008]]. Led by their manager, [[Alex Ferguson]], and team captain, [[Ryan Giggs]], the Manchester United team then ascended the specially constructed rostrum to collect their runners-up medals from [[UEFA]] president [[Michel Platini]] and secretary [[David Taylor (football administrator)|David Taylor]].<ref>Simons, Archibald (1:40:43–1:41:12)</ref> Other dignitaries present included Manchester United chief executive [[David Gill (football executive)|David Gill]]; [[The Football Association]]'s president [[Prince William, Duke of Cambridge|Prince William]], and chairman [[David Triesman, Baron Triesman|Lord Triesman]]; Barcelona president [[Joan Laporta]]; [[Juan Carlos I of Spain]]; [[Royal Spanish Football Federation]] president Ángel María Villar; and Italian Prime Minister [[Silvio Berlusconi]]. Ferguson also collected a commemorative plaque from Platini, but immediately handed it to Giggs.<ref>Simons, Archibald (1:41:15–1:41:18)</ref>

[[File:Celebrando la Copa de Campeones 2008-09.jpg|thumb|The Barcelona team, celebrating their victory with the [[European Champion Clubs' Cup]].|alt=A man in a blue-and-red shirt lifts a large silver trophy above his head, while other men in blue-and-red shirts look on from the background.]]

After offering his commiserations to Manchester United's players,<ref>Simons, Archibald (1:41:27–1:42:34)</ref> Joan Laporta then joined the Barcelona team to lead them up the podium, closely followed by manager Guardiola.<ref>Simons, Archibald (1:42:34–1:43:06)</ref> Laporta himself collected Barcelona's commemorative plaque before accepting a medal from Platini.<ref>Simons, Archibald (1:43:22–1:43:29)</ref> Once the entire Barcelona team had collected their medals, Platini made his way to the front of the podium to present [[Carles Puyol]] with the [[European Champion Clubs' Cup]].<ref>Simons, Archibald (1:46:00–1:46:10)</ref> The Barcelona captain promptly held the trophy aloft with accompaniment from the [[UEFA Champions League Anthem]] and explosions of golden confetti, before leading his team on a lap of honour.<ref>Simons, Archibald (1:46:12–1:47:00)</ref>

===Reaction===
[[File:Lionel Messi 31mar2007.jpg|thumb|left|upright|Barcelona's [[Lionel Messi]] was voted as [[man of the match]] by ''UEFA.com'' users.|alt=A young man wearing a blue-and-red shirt. His hair is held back by a hairband.]]
{{Quote box |quote=We're not the best team in Barça history but we've had the best season. |source=Josep Guardiola, 27 May 2009<ref name="guardiola_salutes"/> |width=206px }}
During the match, users on ''UEFA.com'' were able to vote for their [[man of the match]]; the public vote went to the scorer of Barcelona's second goal, [[Lionel Messi]].<ref name="messi"/> The UEFA Technical Study Group, however, chose the man who provided the cross for Messi's goal, [[Xavi (footballer, born 1980)|Xavi]], as their man of the match, citing his control of the tempo of the match as the reason for their decision.<ref name="xavi"/> Barcelona manager Pep Guardiola took the time to give special praise to Messi, whom he said he moved back into the midfield to increase the team's effectiveness when in possession of the ball.<ref name="praise">{{cite news |title=Guardiola praises Barcelona bravery |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8071196.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=27 May 2009 |access-date=13 August 2009}}</ref> Messi had been withdrawn from his usual right-wing role into a more central position, while [[Samuel Eto'o]], who had started the match as Barcelona's lone centre-forward, was moved out to the right.<ref name="middle_men">{{cite news |first=David |last=Pleat |title=Middle men to the fore in dictating the rhythm with care |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2009/may/28/barcelona-manchester-united-david-pleat-chalkboard |work=The Guardian |date=28 May 2009 |access-date=13 August 2009}}</ref> Guardiola also lauded the bravery of his entire team, stating that he believed that his team's victory hinged on their hard work and propensity for taking risks in attack,<ref name="praise"/> but he admitted that although his side had had the best season in the history of the club, they were not yet the best Barcelona team.<ref name="guardiola_salutes"/>

{{multiple image |footer=Barcelona's victory hinged on the performances of [[Andrés Iniesta]] (left) and [[Xavi (footballer, born 1980)|Xavi]] (right).<ref name="middle_men"/> |image1=Andrés Iniesta - 001.jpg |width1=100 |alt1=A man wearing long, dark blue shorts and a light blue sweatshirt under an orange bib. |image2=Xavi Hernández - 001.jpg |width2=106 |alt2=A man wearing a light blue sweatshirt and dark blue shorts. }}
In the Manchester United camp, manager [[Alex Ferguson]] admitted that his team had been beaten by the better side and that the game was all but lost after the first goal.<ref name="barca_better">{{cite news |title=Barca were better side |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/m/man_utd/8071178.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=28 May 2009 |access-date=7 September 2009}}</ref> United forward [[Cristiano Ronaldo]], however, criticised his team's tactics, saying that "everything went wrong".<ref>{{cite news |first=Giles |last=Mole |title=Manchester United v Barcelona: Cristiano Ronaldo blames tactics for Rome defeat |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-united/5398293/Manchester-United-v-Barcelona-Cristiano-Ronaldo-blames-tactics-for-Rome-defeat.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-united/5398293/Manchester-United-v-Barcelona-Cristiano-Ronaldo-blames-tactics-for-Rome-defeat.html |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |work=The Daily Telegraph |date=28 May 2009 |access-date=27 April 2011}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Ferguson paid tribute to Messi and the midfield partnership of Xavi and [[Andrés Iniesta|Iniesta]],<ref name="barca_better"/> while [[Wayne Rooney]] lauded Iniesta as "the best player in the world".<ref>{{cite news |first=Giles |last=Mole |title=Manchester United v Barcelona: Wayne Rooney backs Andres Iniesta for Ballon d'Or |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-united/5398387/Manchester-United-v-Barcelona-Wayne-Rooney-backs-Andres-Iniesta-for-Ballon-dOr.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-united/5398387/Manchester-United-v-Barcelona-Wayne-Rooney-backs-Andres-Iniesta-for-Ballon-dOr.html |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |work=The Daily Telegraph |date=28 May 2009 |access-date=27 April 2011}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Ferguson also expressed his regret at the unavailability of midfielder [[Darren Fletcher]], who was suspended for the match, and lamented at his side's "shoddy" defending and ineffectiveness when in possession.<ref name="barca_better"/><ref>{{cite news |first=Jason |last=Burt |title=Manchester United v Barcelona: Reaction |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-united/5396685/Manchester-United-v-Barcelona-Reaction.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220112/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-united/5396685/Manchester-United-v-Barcelona-Reaction.html |archive-date=12 January 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |work=The Daily Telegraph |date=27 May 2009 |access-date=27 April 2011}}{{cbignore}}</ref> Nevertheless, Ferguson paid tribute to Guardiola's achievement of winning [[Continental Treble|the treble]] in his first season of management.<ref name="barca_better"/> Barcelona's victory also made Guardiola only the sixth man to win the competition as both a player and a manager, following in the footsteps of [[Miguel Muñoz]], [[Giovanni Trapattoni]], [[Johan Cruyff]], [[Carlo Ancelotti]] and, most recently, [[Frank Rijkaard]], who also won the Champions League as Barcelona manager.<ref>{{cite news |first=Andrew |last=Haslam |title=Rookie coach enters illustrious company |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01d9-0e731eb0209b-b9cd8f963f76-1000--rookie-coach-enters-illustrious-company/ |publisher=UEFA |date=27 May 2009 |access-date=8 September 2009}}</ref>

{{Quote box |quote=You have to give credit to a very good Barcelona team – the better team won. |source=Sir Alex Ferguson, 27 May 2009<ref name="guardiola_salutes"/> |width=150px |align=left }}

The match turned out to be the last for [[Carlos Tevez]] as a Manchester United player, as he chose not to make his loan spell with the club permanent and joined their local rivals, [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Man Utd announce Tevez departure |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/m/man_utd/8110740.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=20 June 2009 |access-date=7 September 2009}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Tevez completes move to Man City |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/m/man_city/8150002.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=14 July 2009 |access-date=7 September 2009}}</ref> After announcing his intention to leave Manchester United, Tevez criticised Ferguson's team selection for the 2009 Champions League final, saying that Ferguson should have included him in the starting line-up, with the rationale that the match was the only final that Manchester United had lost in his time at the club.<ref>{{cite news |title='Alex Ferguson should have started me against Barcelona,' says Carlos Tevez |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/jun/30/carlos-tevez-champions-league-final |work=The Guardian |date=30 June 2009 |access-date=7 September 2009}}</ref>

The match was also Ronaldo's last game at Manchester United for over twelve years, as he completed a world record £80&nbsp;million transfer to [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] on 1 July.<ref>{{cite news |title=Ronaldo completes £80m Real move |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/m/man_utd/8121951.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=1 July 2009 |access-date=7 September 2009}}</ref> He would later go on to rejoin United in August 2021, and departing once more in November 2022.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cristiano Ronaldo to Man Utd: 'I'm back where I belong' and dedicates return to Sir Alex Ferguson |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/58367537 |work=BBC Sport |date=31 August 2021 |access-date=11 September 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Cristiano Ronaldo to leave Manchester United with immediate effect |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/63720774 |work=BBC Sport |date=22 November 2022 |access-date=23 January 2023}}</ref>
[[File:Tevez throw.jpg|thumb|left|upright|[[Carlos Tevez]] chose to leave Manchester United at the end of his contract in June 2009.|alt=A man wearing a red football shirt, white shorts and black socks. His hair is held back by a hairband. He is holding a yellow football with a purple design.]]

Television audiences for the final reached more than 10&nbsp;million in both Spain and the United Kingdom. 11.3&nbsp;million people watched the match on Spain's [[Antena 3 (Spain)|Antena 3]] network – the largest viewing figures for that station in the past year – while another 600,000 watched on [[Canal+ Spain]]. In the United Kingdom, however, although the match received the highest ratings of any programme that evening, viewing figures were down by 1.5&nbsp;million on [[2008 UEFA Champions League final|the previous year's final]], with just 9.6&nbsp;million watching on ITV1, although an additional 1.79&nbsp;million watched on [[Sky Sports|Sky Sports 1]]. Despite there being no Italian or French representative in the final, the figures were much the same in those two countries as they were in Spain and the United Kingdom, with Italy's [[Rai Uno]] garnering an average viewership of 9.63&nbsp;million and France's [[TF1]] receiving 8.25&nbsp;million. Only 6.55&nbsp;million people watched the match on Germany's [[Sat.1]] channel.<ref>{{cite news |first=Steve |last=Clarke |title=European soccer final wins TV prize |url=https://variety.com/2009/scene/markets-festivals/european-soccer-final-wins-tv-prize-1118004248/ |work=Variety |date=28 May 2009 |access-date=8 September 2009}}</ref> According to a survey, global viewing figures for the match averaged 109&nbsp;million; this put the UEFA Champions League final above the [[Super Bowl]] (106&nbsp;million viewers in [[Super Bowl XLIII|2009]]) as the most-watched annual sporting event in the world. Total figures, which included viewers who watched only part of the match, put the Champions League final even further ahead of the Super Bowl, with 206&nbsp;million viewers compared to 162&nbsp;million.<ref>{{cite news |first=Simon |last=Evans |title=Champions League final tops Super Bowl in TV survey |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/idUSTRE60U19U20100131 |work=Reuters |date=31 January 2010 |access-date=15 April 2010}}</ref>

[[File:Etoo Joan Gamper Trophy 2008.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Samuel Eto'o]] scored the opening goal in the 10th minute of the game.|alt=A black man wearing a blue-and-red halved shirt and blue shorts.]]

In the United Kingdom, [[bookmaker]]s made a profit from bets on a Manchester United victory. [[William Hill (bookmaker)|William Hill]] reported two bets of around £20,000 placed on Manchester United, while [[Paddy Power]] received one of £5,500 and [[Ladbrokes]] one of £3,000. Ladbrokes also took a bet of £10,000 on a Barcelona win, but bookmakers reported that most of the money was put on Manchester United. Extrabet.com paid out £12,000 to a £3,000 bet on Samuel Eto'o to score the first goal at odds of 4–1.<ref>{{cite news |first=Dan |last=Roebuck |title=Barcelona triumph keeps the bookies happy |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2009/may/28/champions-league-final-betting |work=The Guardian |date=28 May 2009 |access-date=8 September 2009}}</ref>

===Rewards===
Both teams received significant financial rewards; for winning the final, Barcelona won €7&nbsp;million in prize money, and Manchester United received €4&nbsp;million as runners-up.<ref name="distribution" /> Despite losing the match, Manchester United earned the most money overall from their 2008–09 Champions League campaign, receiving €38.281&nbsp;million compared to Barcelona's €30.968&nbsp;million, due to their share of the market pool, based on the value of the television market in their home countries.<ref name="distribution"/>

As winners of the UEFA Champions League, Barcelona competed in the [[2009 UEFA Super Cup]], in which they beat [[2008–09 UEFA Cup]] winners [[FC Shakhtar Donetsk|Shakhtar Donetsk]] 1–0 at [[Stade Louis II]] in Monaco on 28 August 2009,<ref name="super_cup"/> and in the [[2009 FIFA Club World Cup]] in [[Abu Dhabi]],<ref name="club_world_cup"/> where they beat [[Estudiantes de La Plata|Estudiantes]] of Argentina in the final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Barcelona beat Estudiantes to win the Club World Cup |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/8422908.stm |work=BBC Sport |date=19 December 2009 |access-date=6 February 2010}}</ref>

